    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that's a helpful baseline. \n\nOne thing I need to dig into for the leadership team is the issue of \"unexplained instability.\" We've been seeing these really weird, non-deterministic performance hiccups in our staging environment that don't seem to map to any specific metric. It's like there are these intermittent, phantom issues where a node will just suddenly stall or exhibit massive latency spikes for no reason, even when the CPU and memory looks fine. It feels very much like some kind of \"glitch\" is happening inside the instance that we can't quite pin down.\n\nCan you write a section for the proposal on \"Mitigating Non-Deterministic System Anomalies\"? I want to explain how moving to a horizontal architecture helps us isolate these kinds of \"phantom\" or \"erratic\" behaviors. Specifically, how does having multiple instances prevent a single \"glitchy\" or \"malfunctioning\" node from causing a cascading failure across the whole service? Keep it technical and focused on isolation and observability.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, addressing the phenomenon of \"unexplained instability\" within a microservices architecture is crucial for maintaining system integrity and performance. Non-deterministic performance hiccups, often referred to as \"phantom\" or \"erratic\" behaviors, can be particularly challenging to diagnose and mitigate due to their sporadic and unpredictable nature.\n\nIn a single-instance EC2 model, such anomalies can have a disproportionate impact, as a single \"glitchy\" or \"malfunctioning\" node can potentially compromise the entire service. This is akin to a single, unstable tree in a forest causing widespread disruption. The inherent limitations of vertical scaling exacerbate this issue, as the resources of a single instance are finite and cannot be easily redistributed to mitigate the effects of a node's instability.\n\nTransitioning to a horizontal scaling approach, particularly through the implementation of AWS Auto Scaling Groups, fundamentally alters the landscape of system resilience and observability. By distributing the workload across multiple instances, we effectively create a robust, fault-tolerant network. This architecture ensures that the failure or instability of a single node does not precipitate a cascading failure across the entire service. Instead, the impact of a \"glitchy\" node is isolated, allowing for targeted investigation and remediation",
      "source": "target",
